WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
Good morning Hank; it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:05.509
So in the 20th century about as many people
died from smallpox as currently live in the

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:06.759
United States of America.

00:00:06.759 --> 00:00:10.520
I bring this up because I don't think it's
irrational to be terrified of infectious diseases

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.620
- they are terrifying.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:16.700
But then again, for the rest of human history,
no one will die from smallpox because we eradicated

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:17.700
it.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.340
Like the beginning of the Wikipedia page for
smallpox is "Smallpox WAS an infectious disease."

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.450
So don't underestimate humans when it comes
to kicking microbial butt.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.640
Today I want to talk about Ebola and what
shouldn't terrify us about it and also what

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.640
should.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.989
So quick update: currently three people have
contracted Ebola in the U.S. Nigeria, which

00:00:33.989 --> 00:00:38.350
had twenty infections, was just declared Ebola
free by the World Health Organization.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:39.350
Good work Nigeria!

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.739
But there have been about ten thousand total
reported cases in Guinea, Liberia, and Sierra

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:44.739
Leone.

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:47.649
Also we know that number is low because there
is a lot of underreporting.

00:00:47.649 --> 00:00:53.019
And as this graph indicates, the rate of infection
is not even beginning to level off - and that

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:54.019
is terrifying.

00:00:54.019 --> 00:00:56.960
The current outbreak here in the United States
is-is not terrifying.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.199
For one thing infection controls are good
enough that the disease won't spread widely.

00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:04.320
And for another, the survival rate so far
is over 80% thanks to excellent supportive

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.870
care, whereas in West Africa it's closer to
30%.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:11.579
But Hank as long as the outbreak is worsening
in West Africa, there will be other outbreaks

00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:15.600
around the world regardless of whether we
revoke the visas of people traveling from

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.220
affected countries and regardless of whether
every nation on Earth checks every international

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.370
traveler for fever.

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:25.399
Hank, microorganisms just aren't very well
informed when it comes to the borders of nation

00:01:25.399 --> 00:01:26.399
states.

00:01:26.399 --> 00:01:29.570
And if we're going to enjoy the many benefits
of globalization - inexpensive Argentinian

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:34.439
beef in our Big Macs, West African chocolate
in our Hershey's Kisses, roses from Kenya,

00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:38.259
clothes from Bangladesh - if we're going to
have all that stuff we're also going to live

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:39.890
in a global health community.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:44.329
We like to imagine that deadly infectious
diseases like cholera or malaria happen to

00:01:44.329 --> 00:01:47.390
other people, to like poor people who live
far away from us.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.710
But Ebola reminds us just how false that us/them
dichotomy is.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:55.759
Like better health care infrastructure in
Liberia is not just good for the health and

00:01:55.759 --> 00:02:00.450
productivity of Liberians, it's also good
for the health and productivity of all humans.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.890
And then there's the non-Ebola effects of
Ebola, like as Bill Gates has pointed out,

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:07.579
the collapse of public health care systems
in Sierra Leone and Liberia and Guinea has

00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:11.170
probably led to more deaths from non-Ebola
causes than from Ebola.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:15.640
Harvests are being affected as is every segment
of these countries' economies, and we're probably

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.220
at the beginning of the outbreak.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.350
I mean the World Health Organization is estimating
that by December, there could be between five

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.440
and ten thousand new cases of Ebola per WEEK.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.580
Now Hank I understand the impulse to try to
cut these countries off from the rest of the

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:29.580
world.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:33.170
But experts agree that will lead to more Ebola
around the world, not less, as we've already

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:37.110
seen from the insufficiently robust international
response to the initial outbreak.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:38.110
So what are we going to do?

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.490
Well, the World Health Organization's current
plan is 70-70-60.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.330
Although grammatically I would say that it's
more sixty, seventy, seventy.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:48.300
The idea is that within sixty days, seventy
percent of Ebola patients will be isolated

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.490
and seventy percent of burials will be safe.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:54.540
That's going to require tremendous community
outreach and lots more hospital beds which

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:58.980
is going to be very expensive, like up to
a billion dollars a month and also will require

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.330
a lot of staff.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:04.441
The heroes of this story will be the people
who build and run those clinics, and they

00:03:04.441 --> 00:03:09.620
will be American and Cuban and Botswanan,
and there will be many, many West Africans

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:13.360
who work together to isolate and treat Ebola
patients.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.880
That is hugely courageous work, and it is
done on behalf of all humans.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:22.030
It will eventually be effective, and the Ebola
outbreak will eventually be contained but

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:23.100
at a very high cost.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.750
But Hank, I hope that we might learn from
this disaster than when it comes to communicable

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:31.610
diseases in today's world, threats to the
health of any humans are threats to the health

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:32.680
of all humans.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:33.720
I'll see you on Friday.

